ipts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/20260924-capture-input/native.log","scripts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/20260924-capture-input/receipt.json","scripts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/20260924-capture-input/report.json","scripts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/20260924-capture-input/report.md","scripts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/20260924-capture-input/run.json","scripts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/20260926-demo-app/hitches.xml","scripts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/20260926-demo-app/potential-hangs.xml","scripts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/20260926-demo-app/report.json","scripts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/20260926-demo-app/report.md","scripts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/20260926-demo-app/rss.jsonl","scripts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/20260926-demo-app/run.json"]},{"sha":"102522bc25adc146d4b8cc3f343677ed8170f636","title":"Clarify Loopflow’s first steps and use an installed coding agent (#1297)","files":["LICENSE","README.md","docs/agent-api.md","docs/architecture.html","docs/conducting.md","docs/config.md","docs/getting-started.md","docs/glossary.md","docs/index.md","docs/troubleshooting.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/agent.rs","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/ops/skill/init.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/config.rs","rust/loopflow/src/engine/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/ops/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/run.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/util.rs","swift/LoopflowMac/LoopflowApp.swift","wave/growth/GOAL.md","wave/growth/MEMORY.md","website/content.yaml","website/main.py","website/static/loopflow-full.svg","website/static/loopflow-steps.svg","website/static/style.css","website/tests/e2e/test_download.py"]}],"merged_prs":[{"number":1277,"title":"Mac workspace: navigate repo → Wave → Task → Session in one outline with Task Flow, Monitor, comments, and named Sessions","body":"The Mac app used to open on a flat Sessions queue, with Work on a separate surface and Projects as a navigation tier, so finding the conversation for a Task, seeing where its Flow stood, or checking what was running meant switching screens and reading CLI output. This PR replaces that with one outline and a retained workspace: select a Task to see its Flow, recent Runs, comments and live Runs, then enter a named Session without losing terminals or unfinished input.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Join current Project/KR/Task planning to native Sessions · LOO-291](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-291/unify-wave-navigation-sessions-and-task-monitor-in-one-native)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 leaves the Task open for another serial PR.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n**Mac workspace**\n\n- One sidebar outline per repository: repo → Wave → Task → Session, with Compact, Full hierarchy and Sessions presentations. Started Tasks appear even before they have a Session; unattributed conversations stay reachable under **Orphan sessions**. Search, scroll, expansion and selection survive repository switches.\n- A single breadcrumb toolbar replaces the Podium console and Sessions button. It drills Wave → Task → Session, links the issue ID to Linear, renames the Session in place, shows its Flow step and iteration, and carries the worktree chip, **Complete**, Activity and Monitor.\n- Task overview draws the Flow from a shared read: a preview with **Start** before the Task begins; afterwards the pinned definition, current occurrence and each loop's iteration, with **Resume** and a confirmed **Stop & restart…**. Disabled controls show Rust's reason.\n- **Recent runs** loads on demand. **Comments (n)** reads the Task's Linear thread and keeps the earlier thread marked **May be out of date** with **Retry** when a read fails.\n- **Monitor** opens as a pane beside Sessions and shells in the same multiplexer and shows the selected Task's active Runs, updating automatically. It never reports confirmed emptiness from incomplete evidence.\n- Selecting a Session in another checkout restores that worktree's conversation, companion terminals, split layout and focus. **New conversation** opens a scoped interactive prompt without creating a Task or starting its Flow.\n- Visual pass: warm light palette, a surface ladder instead of shadows, a seven-step type ramp, no pane header with one pane, no colored focus borders. Removing the borders also removed the pane color allocator and its undo bookkeeping. Light mode only.\n- Terminals fall back to timer rendering when macOS cannot provide Ghostty's display link.\n\n**CLI and shared reads**\n\n- `lf runs --active [--task ID] [--json]` reports current provider-backed Runs and observation gaps from verified processes. `--watch --json` (macOS) keeps one reader alive and streams snapshots; Linux supports one-shot reads.\n- `lf session rename <id> \"Name\"`; agents pass `--suggest`, which preserves a human-chosen name.\n- `lf pm task comments --id ID --json` reads the complete comment thread.\n- `lf ls --current` excludes abandoned and retired registrations. `lf work forget wave <id>` removes an empty abandoned registration, with `--dry-run`.\n- A launch inside a Task's worktree without `--task`/`--wave`/`--as` now belongs to that Task.\n- `lf --task ID` accepts direct Flows and inline prompts as well as skills. `design` and `ship-5whys` are skills only; their single-step Flow wrappers are removed. Bare names prefer the skill; select with `lf skill <name>` or `lf flow <name>`.\n- `lf install promote --reuse-home` restores a retained development Home, and adopts a release receipt when that Home already applied the exact draft SQL.\n- Every `SessionRecord` carries a required `runId`. Rust projects Session action labels, unavailable reasons and Work paths; the Mac app dispatches them.\n\n**Measurement and docs**\n\n- `scripts/desktop_performance.py` runs two opt-in desktop journeys (outline interaction and Task workspace readiness) against synthetic data at two po","files":[],"additions":33384,"deletions":3564,"changed_files":196,"merge_commit":"4cd64be3d0432aa04dd9256293eef7088db97ccc"},{"number":1283,"title":"Continue Tasks through review, revision, and saved Flow recovery","body":"Tasks previously stopped after individual autonomous steps, leaving continuation to another launch. This PR drives the saved Flow through successive steps and revision passes, carrying review feedback forward until human input, a blocker, interruption, or Flow completion.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add `lf task advance` and resumable ordinary Flows, sharing cursor transitions and captured definitions—including nested XOR branches—so recovery preserves the selected work and direction.\n- Make `feature` run design review followed by `pursue`: implementation, compression, behavioral and concept reviews, explicit loop decisions, and an interactive demo. Demo feedback can send work through another implementation pass.\n- Replace human Approve/Iterate controls with **Complete** in the CLI and Sessions UI. Completion returns feedback; the following decision step chooses navigation. Blocked decisions reuse one Ask and reassess its answer.\n- Apply decisions only after their originating Run succeeds, preserve completed feedback across recovery, and reject stale worker settlement.\n- Keep development Session reopen commands on the development executable and stop stored Codex OAuth from overriding native login.\n\nExisting invocations retain their captured definitions; obsolete human navigation policies require a fresh invocation. Finishing a Flow does not implicitly merge its PR or complete its Task. Interrupted external operations still require inspection before retry.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded local evidence in [Task continuation evidence](.lf/directions/task-continuation.md) covers continuation, recovery, feedback handoff, Swift Session completion, and Ask fixtures without an installed `lf`, plus formatting and Clippy. Provider/PM effects were simulated. These checks were not rerun for this description; full CI and a live demonstration of the revised demo-to-decision loop remain unverified.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed for this description: run `lf task advance <issue>` for a Task with a saved Flow. It should continue to its next stopping point. At a demo Session, record a concrete revision, have the agent mark the feedback ready, then select **Complete**. The following decision should use that feedback to choose another implementation pass or finish the Flow.","files":[],"additions":7861,"deletions":1859,"changed_files":79,"merge_commit":"5bcc40fdff810c39885a31b3fbfd6557f49fde93"},{"number":1285,"title":"build(deps): bump the rust group across 1 directory with 7 updates","body":"Bumps the rust group with 7 updates in the / directory:\n\n| Package | From | To |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [clap](https://github.com/clap-rs/clap) | `4.6.6` | `4.6.7` |\n| [fancy-regex](https://github.com/fancy-regex/fancy-regex) | `0.19.0` | `0.19.2` |\n| [uuid](https://github.com/uuid-rs/uuid) | `1.24.0` | `1.26.1` |\n| [rand](https://github.com/rust-random/rand) | `0.10.2` | `0.10.3` |\n| [ipnet](https://github.com/krisprice/ipnet) | `2.12.1` | `2.12.2` |\n| [reqwest](https://github.com/seanmonstar/reqwest) | `0.13.4` | `0.13.5` |\n| [tower-http](https://github.com/tower-rs/tower-http) | `0.7.0` | `0.7.1` |\n\n\nUpdates `clap` from 4.6.6 to 4.6.7\n<details>\n<summary>Release notes</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/releases\">clap's releases</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>v4.6.7</h2>\n<h2>[4.6.7] - 2026-09-14</h2>\n<h3>Features</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><em>(derive)</em> Add <code>#[command(defer = &lt;bool&gt;)]</code> attribute to opt-in to lazy initialisation of subcommands</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Changelog</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md\">clap's changelog</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>[4.6.7] - 2026-09-14</h2>\n<h3>Features</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><em>(derive)</em> Add <code>#[command(defer = &lt;bool&gt;)]</code> attribute to opt-in to lazy initialisation of subcommands</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Commits</summary>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/d3e59a9ab214910b9dad02921b7ef42c6400de9b\"><code>d3e59a9</code></a> chore: Release</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/d997f878c484e02b2935d32a7b67afe990e91227\"><code>d997f87</code></a> docs: Update changelog</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/fb6058cc38cf6f4b07b8db89c850fc80e059e222\"><code>fb6058c</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/clap-rs/clap/issues/6409\">#6409</a> from heaths/pwsh-support</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/2310870f7a9fe4c39f5bb5620261a06ac9aaa0b1\"><code>2310870</code></a> test(complete): Add tests for completer_for_path</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/5967c17393a09f90112cdc5b2c20a1383f0bd8e4\"><code>5967c17</code></a> refactor(complete): Move shell detection to Shells</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/594602bb29f374e49da5029df63e5c740c723ad0\"><code>594602b</code></a> fix(complete): Detect pwsh for PowerShell</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/3a4f2d031b5110aaaac40f1cb1d6c0b8ff619df8\"><code>3a4f2d0</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/clap-rs/clap/issues/6427\">#6427</a> from clap-rs/renovate/shlex-2.x</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/67ebaed4ac95a83d5d7cc22d64ee0fc162ae1db3\"><code>67ebaed</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/clap-rs/clap/issues/6426\">#6426</a> from clap-rs/renovate/actions-checkout-7.x</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/c968b136d0e6f560c08b06a844a20f2798a00096\"><code>c968b13</code></a> chore(deps): Update Rust crate shlex to v2</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/8f247cbf58c582a755ee4dbe62a2390d93b9f8eb\"><code>8f247cb</code></a> chore(deps): Update actions/checkout action to v7</li>\n<li>Additional commits viewable in <a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/compare/clap_complete-v4.6.6...clap_complete-v4.6.7\">compare view</a></li>\n</ul>\n</details>\n<br />\n\nUpdates `fancy-regex` from 0.19.0 to 0.19.2\n<details>\n<summary>Release notes</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/fancy-regex/fancy-regex/releases\">fancy-regex's releases</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>0.19.2</h2>\n<h3>Added</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Add a <code>leftmost_longest</code> feature (off by default) that adds a method to the <code>RegexBuilder</code>/<code>RegexOptionsBuilder</code> for building a VM which would operate in leftmost-longest match mode instead of the regular leftmost-first mode. Useful for POSIX compliance. (<a href=\"https://","files":[],"additions":22,"deletions":22,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"4ba6569f93e3a26d93cbe6f0a52a82e0f8d867f0"},{"number":1287,"title":"Recover blocked PR landings and focus customer prompts","body":"PR landing can now retry a blocked operation and recover on the same commit while showing the repair agent's concrete blocker. Customer prompts also become smaller, with specialized instructions delivered by the skills that use them.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Remove the landing-only launch restriction, lifetime repair ceiling, one-shot incident admission and required SHA change. Reconcile fresh GitHub evidence after every repair; authoritative merge wins over provider errors.\n- Resume blocked landings under the existing generation machinery. Retain the supervisor's operation lock through canceled waiters, preserve active checkout placement, and carry Task disposition through repair publication.\n- Reuse Run final answers for `published`/`blocked` results and CLI repair output. Remove duplicate supervision fields and drop the unused repair counter through a forward migration.\n- Reduce universal operating guidance by 71.8%; move control procedures into their owning skills. Retire stale repository workflows and the obsolete export probe, retain chapter history, and preserve lessons in their existing owners.\n\n## Checks\n\n- 389 affected Rust tests passed, covering engine behavior, landing recovery/takeover, Task settlement, populated migration preservation, prompt goldens and CLI recovery.\n- 75 focused Python checks and two documentation checks passed.\n- Formatting, Clippy (`-D warnings`), architecture ownership, migration history, Python lint and shell checks passed.\n\n## Limits\n\nGitHub and the provider are simulated in local recovery proofs. Live-provider permissions, hosted repair/interruption, lfd HTTP delivery and orphan-provider termination remain unproven. Green-but-unmergeable PRs and the original Etude Task-attribution discrepancy are outside this change. The design's hosted acceptance check remains outstanding.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested on an authorized PR: resolve a reported external blocker and rerun `lf pr land` without creating an empty commit. The landing resumes, displays repair conclusions, and finishes when GitHub confirms merge; another concrete blocker is returned with its required action. Preserve the intended Task disposition with `-c` or `--next <slug>`.\n\n\n## CI repair\n\nThe failed head `c8c5c0c7490e4e56172f4620214fd535ba791202` exposed a stale launch-prompt assertion and a terminal test that could time out using its pre-sleep block count. Launch coverage now checks the canonical document; terminal polling reads fresh state before checking its unchanged deadline. TESTING.md records the corresponding local checks.\n\nA temporary four-second wake-up delay reproduced the terminal failure before the fix and passed afterward; the test retains its original 20 ms polling interval. Local verification passed 8 launch tests, 14 builtin tests, 12 terminal tests, Cargo formatting, all-target Clippy with warnings denied, and Swift boundary checks. The full Swift attempt passed the failing terminal case but later stalled and was terminated; it is not counted as a full-suite pass. The repaired head still requires hosted CI.\n\nThe Xcode fallback app and test runners also compiled successfully with `build-for-testing`. Review found no production behavior change or added dependency; prompt content remains owned by the builtin contract tests.","files":[],"additions":1755,"deletions":3009,"changed_files":55,"merge_commit":"9f2e27f05209c0b82156124d2e6a024c02d8c8e2"},{"number":1288,"title":"Preserve people’s names in requests and saved artifacts","body":"Saved decisions could leave readers unsure who requested what, and chat history lacked author names. This PR carries preferred names and request authors into agent context so Tasks, PRs, and summaries can name the right person while conversation still uses “you.”\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add personal `user.name` configuration and `lf name --json`. Carry the caller’s name through launches and SSH; send participant updates on native resume, including clearing a stale name.\n- Preserve request authors through CLI and Mac chat, journal replay, Discord, and Linear. Keep provider identity separate from display names and distinguish requesters from publishers or editors.\n- Update shared guidance, builtin skills, and documentation to use names in saved artifacts while preserving conversational transcripts and historical quotations.\n- Consolidate local chat delivery into one fallible path that journals messages before broadcasting them. Bare interrupts remain independent of name lookup.\n\nUnknown authors remain unknown; names do not establish authorization. Decisions and dated evidence are retained in [.lf/name-attribution.md](.lf/name-attribution.md).\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded branch verification passed 799 affected Rust tests, 229 Python tests, 78 website tests, 112 focused Swift tests, Rust formatting/clippy, architecture and Swift boundary checks, and the Mac build. Three website viewport checks were skipped. These results were reviewed, not rerun for this description.\n\nThe full Swift package run stalled and was terminated; a complete pass remains unproven. Live native review-session continuation, native rendering, and hosted UI remain unverified.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough: set `user.name` in personal configuration, then ask a fresh session to write a decision summary and reply conversationally. The summary should use the configured name; the reply should use “you.” Change the preference and repeat—the new summary should use the corrected name without reassigning historical requests.\n\nRecorded fresh CLI generations demonstrated this distinction and preserved unknown attribution. Those were generated examples, not live Task writes.","files":[],"additions":2165,"deletions":882,"changed_files":159,"merge_commit":"9f9f9332fd0cf52adfdb5452f362cc763fe6220c"},{"number":1289,"title":"Recover branch publication and keep learnings in Wave memory","body":"Rebase recreates deleted remote branches despite stale tracking refs, while leases continue protecting unseen remote work. Preparing a replacement PR head now explicitly dequeues queued PRs; GitHub CLI's --disable-auto command returns success without dequeuing them.\n\nFresh landing fixtures use the ephemeral store constructor, and fixture lessons live in infrastructure Wave memory. The memory skills resolve an unnamed Wave from repository objectives and create one repo-wide Wave when none exists. Authoring guidance no longer sends learning notes to .lf/.\n\nChecks: deleted-branch and queued-publication regressions, the authoritative-merge watcher, 14 builtin prompt checks, architecture checks, formatting, and clippy pass. Local transport tests use bare remotes with simulated GitHub/provider responses. Live GitHub branch recreation succeeded. The landing suite passed 19 scenarios; concurrent recompilation invalidated one running executable, and that repeat-landing scenario passed in isolation.","files":[],"additions":327,"deletions":119,"changed_files":12,"merge_commit":"2eaa8c4a96e0a37fa0f756e1a994cf56a65c1f7e"},{"number":1290,"title":"Pair minor releases with closing patches and full-cycle notes","body":"Minor releases now summarize the completed cycle and share their product snapshot with its closing patch. If changes remain, publish the next patch first; otherwise reuse the latest completed patch. Patch notes compare with the preceding release, while minor notes compare with the preceding .0 tag.\n\n- Persist the selected patch/minor pair so retries finish publication without selecting new versions or stopping at the no-change check.\n- Add `lf release notes VERSION --preview` without changing release archives or manifests. Existing versions end at their historical tag and use historical context.\n- Bound PR discovery by the release range and retain coverage across the cycle before trimming source detail.\n\nA minor stops before tagging if the merged candidate differs from its prepared snapshot. Pair recovery uses a local controller receipt; this does not add cross-machine handoff.\n\nChecks: 109 focused release/builtin tests passed for the paired-release implementation; 56 release integration tests passed after historical preview support; seven affected source/preview tests passed after the final query adjustment. Cargo formatting and Clippy with warnings denied pass. Publication tests simulate GitHub/provider boundaries; no release was published.\n\nObserved previews: v0.13.0 since v0.12.0, v0.12.0 since v0.11.0, and v0.11.0 since v0.10.0 completed through the configured notes provider. The two historical previews retained all 187 and 61 commits and matching PRs respectively; original archives remained unchanged.","files":[],"additions":994,"deletions":91,"changed_files":10,"merge_commit":"1549fcc3fe50366d2a38b5eba1cca2a12a7bc234"},{"number":1291,"title":"Run Wave governance as single attempts and remove tmux desktop integration","body":"Waves currently carry a second Flow interpreter and send its cursor to the app just to describe attempts. This change runs one `wave/operate` attempt per wake and keeps failures, input claims and provider sessions in the turn journal, reducing the execution machinery operators and maintainers must understand.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Remove Wave stack traversal, queue/skip controls, the subprocess fallback and playhead HTTP/SSE/client state. Tasks and ordinary Flows retain their execution engines and captured definitions.\n- Preserve historical journals with an explicit cutover disposition. `lf wave recover <name>` displays the original continuation; cancellation requires its exact source sequence and a reason. Unknown provider liveness blocks replacement.\n- Present failures from recorded attempts. Independent scheduled successes cannot erase earlier failures; historical retries retain their attribution.\n- Remove the tmux plugin and desktop tmux shells. Task terminals use Ghostty with a pool owned by each workspace; Session and background-launch behavior is unchanged.\n\nThe diff removes 1,698 net lines outside scratch, including docs and tests. Shared invocation types move to the engine; historical decoding remains read-only.\n\n## Limitations\n\nUnclosed attempts still need authoritative termination reconciliation, and this PR does not add that command or transfer arbitrary old queues into ordinary Flows. Listener and resident must use matching binaries. Configured live-provider/client acceptance remains outstanding; deterministic transport fixtures are simulated evidence.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Rust: 1,988 passed, 6 skipped in the materialized graph with isolated Home; formatting and all-target Clippy passed.\n- Python: 229 passed. Website: 78 passed, 3 skipped. Swift: 267 passed, plus platform boundaries, macOS build and eight distinct fixture renders.\n- Xcode fallback app/test-runner compilation passed, closing the prior resource-blocked verification gap. Architecture and whitespace checks passed.\n\nThe default local gate remains red on an unchanged Flow Session fixture: its private `LF_HOME` conflicts with the materializer's `LF_CONTROL_HOME`. The same compiled test fails with that pin and passes without it; the full Rust pass above removes the pin. `TESTING.md` records the requirement. This gate does not change the unrelated fixture or claim a default-runner pass. Hosted UI acceptance and configured live provider/client execution remain outstanding.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested configured acceptance: start a Wave, deliver a Work observation and observe one governance turn followed by idle. Chat should reply independently during governance. Interrupt a running attempt and confirm its reason remains visible after reconnect; a later unrelated successful wake must not label it recovered. In a Task workspace, open two shell tabs, switch between them, and confirm each retains its shell.\n","files":[],"additions":2050,"deletions":3748,"changed_files":72,"merge_commit":"1a691ac6a222b95c46859c9c06d162d6442950a4"},{"number":1293,"title":"Remove obsolete directions and orphaned .lf memory","body":"Remove the retired `.lf/directions/` directory and orphaned `.lf/name-attribution.md`. Curate useful continuation, attribution, and feedback lessons into the existing Wave memories, preserving historical evidence and unresolved acceptance gaps.\n\nChapter archives remain unchanged in `.lf/chapters/`. Active configuration, the telemetry flow, and ignored runtime state remain intact.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Repository architecture scan and 20 architecture tests passed during cleanup.\n- Chapter archive relocation and its associated code/skill edits were reverted; all 1,141 archived files retain their original content.\n- Naming conventions and the prohibition on fallback `.lf/` memory already live in existing guidance.","files":[],"additions":148,"deletions":407,"changed_files":5,"merge_commit":"52dbd45589420995b4202e84f8761a91032d44ce"},{"number":1294,"title":"release: v0.12.22","body":"# v0.12.22\n\n<!-- loopflow:release-notes=narrative;gate=safe -->\n\nv0.12.22 keeps work moving through review, revision, and delivery without requiring a fresh launch at every step. Tasks continue their saved Flows, blocked PR landings can recover on the same commit, and Waves run one governance attempt per wake. Requests also carry names into agent context so saved decisions can preserve who asked for what.\n\n## Continue from review to the next useful step\n\n`lf task advance <issue>` drives a Task's saved Flow until it needs input, encounters a blocker or interruption, or finishes. Recovery retains captured definitions, selected branches, and completed review feedback.\n\n- The `feature` Flow runs design review followed by implementation, compression, behavioral and concept reviews, explicit loop decisions, and an interactive demo. Demo feedback can send work through another implementation pass.\n- **Complete** replaces Approve/Iterate in CLI and desktop Sessions. Completing the Session returns feedback; the following decision step chooses where the Flow goes next.\n- Blocked decisions reuse one Ask and reassess its answer. Decisions take effect only after their originating Run succeeds, and stale workers cannot settle newer work.\n- Ordinary Flows are resumable too. Finishing a Flow does not implicitly merge its PR or complete its Task.\n\n## Recover delivery without an empty commit\n\nAfter resolving an external blocker, rerun `lf pr land` on the same head. Landing now reconciles fresh GitHub evidence after repairs and reports the repair agent's concrete conclusion.\n\n- Blocked landings can resume without a lifetime repair ceiling or a required SHA change. GitHub's confirmed merge takes precedence over provider errors.\n- Repair publication preserves active checkout placement and the intended Task disposition, including `-c` or `--next <slug>`.\n- Rebase recreates deleted remote branches despite stale tracking refs, while leases continue protecting unseen remote work.\n- Preparing a replacement PR head explicitly removes the PR from GitHub's merge queue before publication.\n\n## See what happened during Wave operation\n\nEach Wave wake now runs one `wave/operate` attempt, with failures, input claims, and provider sessions recorded in the turn journal. Tasks and ordinary Flows retain their execution engines and captured definitions.\n\n- Wave stack traversal, queue/skip controls, and playhead state are removed. A later unrelated successful wake cannot erase an earlier recorded failure.\n- `lf wave recover <name>` displays the original historical continuation. Canceling it requires its exact source sequence and a reason; unknown provider liveness blocks replacement.\n- Desktop Task terminals use Ghostty with a terminal pool owned by each workspace. The tmux plugin and desktop tmux shells are removed.\n\n## Preserve who requested the work\n\nPersonal `user.name` configuration and request-author context help agents name people in Tasks, PRs, and summaries while keeping direct conversation conversational. Unknown authors remain unknown, and display names do not establish authorization.\n\n- Inspect the configured name with `lf name --json`. Names travel through launches and SSH; native resume updates participant names and clears stale ones.\n- Request authors survive CLI and Mac chat, journal replay, Discord, and Linear without being reassigned to publishers or editors.\n- Specialized operating instructions now live in the skills that use them, reducing universal prompt guidance. Durable lessons live in Wave memory; retired direction files and orphaned attribution notes are removed, while chapter archives remain in `.lf/chapters/`.\n\n## Operational notes\n\n- Existing Flow invocations retain their captured definitions. Start a fresh invocation to replace obsolete review navigation policies. Inspect interrupted external operations before retrying them.\n- Wave listeners and residents must use matching binaries. Historical queues are not automatically transferred into ordinary Flows. Unclosed attempts still require authoritative termination reconciliation; this ","files":[],"additions":110,"deletions":42,"changed_files":10,"merge_commit":"8ae6c6421978e0b013ec9abe34189f8dcfa651e2"},{"number":1295,"title":"Resume sessions without injecting prompts and use Git names by default","body":"Reopening or moving a session now resumes the existing conversation without injecting a participant-update prompt. A missing Loopflow name preference no longer tells the agent to disregard a name it already knows.\n\n- Resolve the display name from a personal Loopflow override, then Git's configured `user.name`, including repository overrides.\n- Replace `lf name` with `lf user name [--json]` and update the Mac chat caller.\n- Keep display names separate from provider IDs. In prose, use a familiar name already known in the conversation, such as Jack; no first-name or username fields are added.\n- Update setup guidance, API descriptions, and documentation.\n\n## Checks\n\n36 focused Rust and Swift tests passed during implementation. The final wording passed all nine focused name/session tests; formatting and clippy across all targets passed. The README/index synchronization check now passes after moving the command example out of the shared introduction. The architecture map check passed.\n\nObserved: the built `lf user name --json` returned `\"Jack Heart\"` from the actual Git configuration. Resume checks use fake provider processes; a live provider reopen demo was not performed.","files":[],"additions":191,"deletions":101,"changed_files":16,"merge_commit":"a48eeb6aed383123c51a6c6cc673591ae19b1417"},{"number":1297,"title":"Clarify Loopflow’s first steps and use an installed coding agent","body":"Newcomers had to decode Loopflow’s terminology before understanding how to start, and unconfigured runs assumed Codex was installed. This PR explains the plan–build–review workflow in plain language, puts the Mac app first, and lets unconfigured runs use an available coding agent.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Rework the homepage around one task, with diagrams showing review points and retry loops. Align the README and docs introduction, add a glossary, and explain prerequisites and provider costs.\n- Lead the download page and Conducting guide with the Mac app while retaining command-line instructions.\n- Select the first installed agent in order: Codex, Claude Code, OpenCode. Explicit configuration and skill defaults retain precedence; missing-agent errors provide installation guidance.\n- Replace the Mac app’s beep when opening a non-Git folder with an explanation and recovery instructions.\n- Add the MIT license and preserve Jack’s positioning decisions and remaining onboarding work in the growth Wave’s goal and memory.\n\nThis improves discovery and initial setup; it does not provide a complete onboarding wizard or remove the Git, Linear, and GitHub prerequisites for Task delivery.\n\n## Checks\n\nReviewed the diff against `main`. Added agent-selection coverage and updated the download-page assertion; tests and lint were not run for this description.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed: open the homepage and follow Download to see the task workflow, Mac app download, and prerequisites. In the Mac app, choose a folder without Git; an alert should explain how to select or initialize a repository.","files":[],"additions":965,"deletions":228,"changed_files":26,"merge_commit":"102522bc25adc146d4b8cc3f343677ed8170f636"}],"decisions":null,"previous_release_notes":"# v0.12.22\n\n<!-- loopflow:release-notes=narrative;gate=safe -->\n\nv0.12.22 keeps work moving through review, revision, and delivery without requiring a fresh launch at every step. Tasks continue their saved Flows, blocked PR landings can recover on the same commit, and Waves run one governance attempt per wake. Requests also carry names into agent context so saved decisions can preserve who asked for what.\n\n## Continue from review to the next useful step\n\n`lf task advance <issue>` drives a Task's saved Flow until it needs input, encounters a blocker or interruption, or finishes. Recovery retains captured definitions, selected branches, and completed review feedback.\n\n- The `feature` Flow runs design review followed by implementation, compression, behavioral and concept reviews, explicit loop decisions, and an interactive demo. Demo feedback can send work through another implementation pass.\n- **Complete** replaces Approve/Iterate in CLI and desktop Sessions. Completing the Session returns feedback; the following decision step chooses where the Flow goes next.\n- Blocked decisions reuse one Ask and reassess its answer. Decisions take effect only after their originating Run succeeds, and stale workers cannot settle newer work.\n- Ordinary Flows are resumable too. Finishing a Flow does not implicitly merge its PR or complete its Task.\n\n## Recover delivery without an empty commit\n\nAfter resolving an external blocker, rerun `lf pr land` on the same head. Landing now reconciles fresh GitHub evidence after repairs and reports the repair agent's concrete conclusion.\n\n- Blocked landings can resume without a lifetime repair ceiling or a required SHA change. GitHub's confirmed merge takes precedence over provider errors.\n- Repair publication preserves active checkout placement and the intended Task disposition, including `-c` or `--next <slug>`.\n- Rebase recreates deleted remote branches despite stale tracking refs, while leases continue protecting unseen remote work.\n- Preparing a replacement PR head explicitly removes the PR from GitHub's merge queue before publication.\n\n## See what happened during Wave operation\n\nEach Wave wake now runs one `wave/operate` attempt, with failures, input claims, and provider sessions recorded in the turn journal. Tasks and ordinary Flows retain their execution engines and captured definitions.\n\n- Wave stack traversal, queue/skip controls, and playhead state are removed. A later unrelated successful wake cannot erase an earlier recorded failure.\n- `lf wave recover <name>` displays the original historical continuation. Canceling it requires its exact source sequence and a reason; unknown provider liveness blocks replacement.\n- Desktop Task terminals use Ghostty with a terminal pool owned by each workspace. The tmux plugin and desktop tmux shells are removed.\n\n## Preserve who requested the work\n\nPersonal `user.name` configuration and request-author context help agents name people in Tasks, PRs, and summaries while keeping direct conversation conversational. Unknown authors remain unknown, and display names do not establish authorization.\n\n- Inspect the configured name with `lf name --json`. Names travel through launches and SSH; native resume updates participant names and clears stale ones.\n- Request authors survive CLI and Mac chat, journal replay, Discord, and Linear without being reassigned to publishers or editors.\n- Specialized operating instructions now live in the skills that use them, reducing universal prompt guidance. Durable lessons live in Wave memory; retired direction files and orphaned attribution notes are removed, while chapter archives remain in `.lf/chapters/`.\n\n## Operational notes\n\n- Existing Flow invocations retain their captured definitions. Start a fresh invocation to replace obsolete review navigation policies. Inspect interrupted external operations before retrying them.\n- Wave listeners and residents must use matching binaries. Historical queues are not automatically transferred into ordinary Flows. Unclosed attempts still require authoritative termination reconciliation; this release does not add that command.\n- Minor releases now share a product snapshot with their closing patch: publish a new patch when changes remain, otherwise reuse the latest completed patch. Patch notes cover the preceding release; minor notes cover the cycle since the preceding `.0` tag. Persisted pair selection supports retries, but recovery receipts remain local to the controller.\n- `lf release notes VERSION --preview` generates notes without changing release archives or manifests. Existing versions use their historical tag and context. Minor publication stops before tagging if the merged candidate differs from the prepared snapshot.\n- Recorded checks include Rust, Python, website, Swift, and Mac build coverage; live GitHub branch recreation and configured release-note previews also succeeded. Landing/provider recovery and release publication proofs used simulations. Configured live provider/client execution and hosted UI acceptance remain outstanding. The default local gate still has a Flow Session fixture conflict between `LF_HOME` and `LF_CONTROL_HOME`; the recorded broad Rust pass used an isolated Home without the conflicting pin.\n\n## Small changes\n\n- Development Session reopen commands retain the development executable.\n- Stored Codex OAuth no longer overrides native login.\n- Terminal test polling reads fresh state before evaluating its deadline.\n- Seven Rust dependencies are updated.","source_limits":{"context_bytes":131072,"commits":256,"merged_prs":200,"files_per_change":100,"area_scopes":32,"name_bytes":256,"title_bytes":300,"path_bytes":512,"pr_body_bytes":4096,"decisions_bytes":16384,"previous_release_notes_bytes":16384,"release_notes_bytes":61440},"omissions":{"commits":0,"merged_prs":0,"commit_files":155,"pr_files":0,"area_scopes":0,"text_bytes":45119,"decisions_bytes":0,"previous_release_notes_bytes":0}}